  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The Pillars of Creation, in the Eagle Nebula, are about 6,500 light-years from Earth.",
      "source": "ESA/Webb release weic2216, 'Webb takes a stunning, star-filled portrait of the Pillars of Creation' (esawebb.org/news/weic2216)",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A light-year is the distance light travels in one year, so light from 6,500 light-years away has been travelling for about 6,500 years.",
      "source": "OpenStax, Astronomy 2e, §1.7 The Universe on the Large Scale (light-year; looking out is looking back in time)",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l6",
        "l8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Light takes about 1.3 seconds to reach us from the Moon and about 8.3 minutes from the Sun.",
      "source": "NASA Science, 'Light-year' / Solar System facts (science.nasa.gov)",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l4",
        "l7"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "simplifications": [
    "6,500 light-years is a rounded distance estimate; published values for the Eagle Nebula vary by several hundred light-years.",
    "Webb photographs in near-infrared. The colours are assigned to infrared wavelengths (shorter is bluer), so this is not what the eye would see.",
    "The scale shows light travel time in seconds on a powers-of-ten axis."
  ],
